  <h1>Rights</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">
    What is quoted, on what basis, and what is and is not claimed.
  </p>

  <h2>The texts quoted here are not ours</h2>

  <p>
    <em>The Book of Discipline of The United Methodist Church</em> is
    © The United Methodist Publishing House. The text of Petition 21039 is the
    work of the General Conference. Wroot Labs claims no copyright in either, and
    nothing on this site should be read as asserting one.
  </p>

  <p>
    This site quotes 28 paragraphs of the Constitution — roughly 1% of the
    Discipline — alongside the amendment that changes them, for the purpose of
    commentary and explanation. The quotation is what makes the commentary
    checkable: an explanation of a legal change that will not show the text
    cannot be verified against anything, which is the specific failing this site
    was built to answer. It is offered free, carries no advertising, and is not
    sold. We consider that fair use. It is not a substitute for the Discipline,
    and anyone who needs the book should buy it from
    <a href="https://www.cokesbury.com">Cokesbury</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the Publishing House would prefer any of it removed or presented
    differently, write to
    <a href="mailto:wilson@wrootlabs.com">wilson@wrootlabs.com</a> and it will be
    done.
  </p>

  <h2>What is ours</h2>

  <p>
    The editorial work is licensed
    <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">CC BY-NC 4.0</a>.
    That covers:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>the plain-English summaries and the "what changed" notes;</li>
    <li>
      the narrative pages — the timeline, the account of what is still undecided,
      the glossary;
    </li>
    <li>
      the generated word-level comparisons, and the segmentation and alignment
      they run on;
    </li>
    <li>
      the mapping between ballot paragraph numbers and Discipline paragraph
      numbers, and the reasoning that established it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    That last one is worth naming plainly, because it is the part that took the
    work. The ballot papers and the Discipline number the same provisions
    differently, and reconciling them meant matching every paragraph by content
    and checking the result against the article numbering. Quoting the
    constitutional text is free. The concordance is the contribution.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>

  <p>
    Where this site and the Book of Discipline differ, the Discipline governs.
    This is not an official publication of The United Methodist Church, the
    General Conference, the Council of Bishops, or any annual conference. If you
    find an error — a misquotation, a wrong paragraph number, a summary that
    misstates what a provision does — <a href="mailto:wilson@wrootlabs.com">say
    so</a> and it will be fixed and the change noted.
  </p>
